

{"id":"5ff17bd0-85c1-4de6-bf9c-737f176b96eb","object":"chat.completion","created":1786439012,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_019\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_08\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 4,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_07\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 5,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_06\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 9,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_03\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 15,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_02\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 9,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task for a UAV integrated-repair optimization. The task requires us to return one JSON object with a record_id and decisions array. Each decision corresponds to a mission, with mission_id, uav_id, held boolean, path_id, depart_slot, charge_blocks. The baseline has five missions: MISSION_01 to MISSION_05. They are assigned to UAV_01 (MISSION_01, MISSION_05) and UAV_02 (MISSION_02, MISSION_03, MISSION_04) in a fixed order per UAV. The instance has FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER, so each UAV gets its missions in the given sequence_index. For UAV_01: MISSION_01 (seq 0), MISSION_05 (seq 1). For UAV_02: MISSION_02 (seq 0), MISSION_03 (seq 1), MISSION_04 (seq 2). We must decide held or not, and for not-held, path_id, depart_slot, charge_blocks (0-3). Heed the normative SOC event order: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. The charge_blocks are after the turnaround, at the destination of the mission. For each UAV we need to schedule departures so that all constraints are satisfied: release slots, due slots, battery energy, reserve, capacity constraints, corridor closures, pad/charger capacity, initial SOC overrides, etc.\n\nFirst, let's list the missions and their parameters. We need to parse the instance details.\n\nUAVs:\n- UAV_01: available_slot 4, battery_capacity 22 kWh, charge_kwh_per_block 0.95, empty_energy_per_km 0.145, initial_soc_kwh: originally 3.802, but intervention overrides to 2.713174. payload_capacity 7 kg, payload_energy_per_km_kg 0.012, reserve_kwh 2.5, max_wind_level 2, start VP_01, travel_time_coefficient 0.86.\n- UAV_02: available_slot 4, battery_capacity 16 kWh, charge_kwh_per_block 1.2, empty_energy_per_km 0.1, initial_soc_kwh 9.635464 (no override), payload_capacity 4 kg, payload_energy_per_km_kg 0.026, reserve_kwh 1.5, max_wind_level 1, start VP_01, travel_time_coefficient 1.0.\n\nMissions:\n\nMISSION_01: UAV_01, origin VP_01, dest VP_04, payload 0.5 kg, priority urgent, release 4, due 6. Path options:\n- PATH_01: distance 1.0 km, energy 0.160815, travel slots 3, max wind 1, corridor COR_08 (wind 1), compatible? The UAV_01 max_wind is 2, so wind 1 is fine. But closures: COR_08 is closed for many slots: 7-27. departure slot + travel slots must avoid closed? Typically closures mean you can't be in the corridor during closed slots. The edge segment duration slots 3, offset_slot 0. So if we depart at slot d, the corridor is occupied from d+0 to d+2 inclusive. For PATH_01, we need slots d, d+1, d+2 to not be closed for COR_08. COR_08 closure slots are 7-27 inclusive. So if d is such that any of d, d+1, d+2 are in [7,27], not allowed. Since release is 4, earliest departure 4. If d=4, then slots 4,5,6. COR_08 closed slots start at 7, so 4,5,6 are open? Check interventions: closed_corridor_slots: \"COR_08\": [7,8,...27]. So 4,5,6 are not in the list. So PATH_01 at d=4 is possible. Travel time 3 slots. Arrival at dest: depart + travel slots = d+3? Typically arrival is at slot = depart_slot + travel_slots. The normative event order: service flight occurs in travel slots. At end of flight, you're at destination. So arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots. Then reserve check, turnaround (1 slot), then post-service charge. Due slot for MISSION_01 is 6. Arrival must be <= due? To meet due, the mission must be served by due slot. In the baseline, depart_slot=4, path PATH_05 (travel slots 2) gave arrival slot = 4+2=6 <=6. With PATH_01 travel 3 slots, arrival slot=4+3=7 > due=6, so violates due. So PATH_01 at d=4 is too late. PATH_01 travel 3 slots needs depart at latest 3? But release is 4, so cannot. So PATH_01 cannot meet due slot 6 if minimum depart is 4. So PATH_01 is infeasible for MISSION_01 due to due slot. PATH_05: travel slots 2, max wind 0, energy 0.1812. Corridor COR_06. Closed slots: COR_06 closed in slots 4-27. That's almost all. Check: closed_corridor_slots includes COR_06 from slot 4 to 27 inclusive. So any departure from slot 4 onward would be during closure. But the mission must depart at 4 earliest. If we depart at 4, corridor COR_06 would be occupied at slots 4 and 5 (travel 2 slots, offset 0). Both slots 4 and 5 are closed. So PATH_05 is closed. PATH_08: travel slots 2, max wind 1, energy 0.144733, corridor COR_03. COR_03 closures: from slots 6 to 27. So if depart at d, slots d and d+1 (travel 2) must be open. If d=4, slots 4,5 are open (closures start at 6). So PATH_08 at d=4 works. Travel slots 2, arrival slot = 6, meets due 6. So PATH_08 is feasible. Baseline chose PATH_05 but it's closed now due to intervention. So we must use PATH_08. So MISSION_01: held false, path_id PATH_08, depart_slot 4, charge_blocks 0. Charge_blocks 0 might be ok; we'll check energy.\n\nNow compute energy for MISSION_01. UAV_01 initial SOC 2.713174 kWh. Need to deduct incoming deadhead: none (incoming_deadhead null). Then service flight: energy consumed for the path. The path provides energy_kwh for that path, which presumably includes empty + payload energy for the distance. Already computed: PATH_08 energy_kwh = 0.144733. Then after service flight, check reserve. Must have SOC after flight >= reserve (2.5). So starting SOC 2.713174 - 0.144733 = 2.568441 kWh > 2.5, so reserve met. Then destination turnaround: 1 slot. Then post-service charge: charge_blocks * charge_kwh_per_block (0.95) added to battery. But charge_blocks can be 0-3. If we set 0, battery remains 2.568441 after turnaround. Then this is the SOC available for next mission MISSION_05. Post-service charge cannot exceed battery capacity: 22 kWh, no issue. Also charger capacity: at destination VP_04, charger count 2, pad capacity 2, but no conflicts likely for this first mission. So charge_blocks 0 is fine. However, energy is low for next mission; we might want to charge to ensure enough for MISSION_05. Let's evaluate MISSION_05.\n\nMISSION_05: UAV_01 sequence_index 1, origin VP_04, dest VP_03, release 9, due 22. Path options:\n- PATH_02: distance 0.8 km, travel slots 1, max wind 0, energy 0.1208, corridor COR_04. Check closures: COR_04 closed slots: 4-8, 10-27. So closed at 4,5,6,7,8, then 10 onward. So slot 9 is open? Check: closed_corridor_slots includes COR_04: \"4\",\"5\",\"6\",\"7\",\"8\",\"10\",\"11\",... So slot 9 is NOT listed, so open. So if we depart at slot 9, the travel slot is 9 (corridor occupied at slot 9), which is open. So PATH_02 is feasible. Baseline depart 9, path PATH_02, arrival slot 10 (9+1). Due 22, ok. So MISSION_05 can be held false with depart 9, path PATH_02.\n\nNow compute energy needed. After MISSION_01 at VP_04, we had 2.568441 kWh. Before MISSION_05, we need to have incoming deadhead from previous mission? Incoming deadhead is null (since this mission's expected_incoming_origin_vertiport is VP_04, and origin is VP_04, so no deadhead). So start with that SOC. Subtract service flight energy 0.1208 -> 2.568441 - 0.1208 = 2.447641. Then check reserve: reserve is 2.5 kWh. 2.447641 < 2.5, so RESERVE VIOLATION. That means we cannot fly MISSION_05 without charging after MISSION_01. So we need to add charging after MISSION_01. So MISSION_01 must have charge_blocks >0 to raise SOC enough. Let's compute required charge to meet reserve after MISSION_05. After MISSION_01, before MISSION_05, we will have SOC0 = 2.568441 (post-flight) + charge_blocks * 0.95. Then we fly MISSION_05, consuming 0.1208, so final = SOC0 + charge*0.95 - 0.1208. Must be >= 2.5 (reserve). So SOC0_min = 2.5 + 0.1208 = 2.6208. Current SOC0 without charge is 2.568441, short by 0.052359. Each charge block adds 0.95, so even 1 block is enough. So charge_blocks = 1 gives SOC0 = 2.568441 + 0.95 = 3.518441. Then after MISSION_05: 3.518441 - 0.1208 = 3.397641 >= 2.5. Also must not exceed battery capacity 22. So 1 charge block suffices. But we must ensure the charging occurs at destination after turnaround. Turnaround takes 1 slot. So MISSION_01 at depart 4, travel slots 2, arrival slot 6. Turnaround slot 6? Actually, event order: incoming deadhead (none) -> service flight (slots 4,5 for travel) -> arrival at slot 6? Let's be precise: the travel_slots = 2 means the flight takes 2 slots. If depart_slot = 4, then the flight occupies slots 4 and 5, arriving at the end of slot 5? In the normative event order, the \"service flight\" is the travel, after which is reserve check, then turnaround. Typically, the arrival slot is depart_slot + travel_slots. The baseline had depart_slot=4, travel_slots=2, and due=6, and they considered arrival at 6 to meet due. So they treat arrival slot as depart_slot + travel_slots. So if depart=4, travel_slots=2, arrival=6. Then reserve check at 6, then turnaround at slot 6 (?) probably also at arrival slot? The order: after arrival, do reserve check, then destination turnaround. Turnaround blocks the UAV for 1 slot at the destination pad. Likely, turnaround occurs in the slot immediately following arrival, or in the same slot? In many AAM problems, turnaround takes T slots starting at the arrival slot. But the task says \"destination turnaround\" after service flight and reserve check. For simplicity, we'll assume turnaround occupies the slot after arrival. So if arrival slot = 6, turnaround occurs in slot 7. Then post-service charge slots occur after turnaround, starting at slot 8? Or at slot 7? Usually charging slots are contiguous blocks after the turnaround. The number of charge_blocks means the UAV occupies a charger for that many slots. So with 1 charge block, it would charge in 1 slot after turnaround. So the UAV becomes free at slot 8. But MISSION_05 release is 9, and it's at VP_04, so it can start at 9. That works. Check pad capacity: at VP_04, pad capacity 2. At slot 7, turnaround occupies a pad for UAV_01. Also other UAVs? UAV_02 will have missions going through VP_04 possibly. We'll need to check conflicts later.\n\nSo MISSION_01: path PATH_08, depart_slot 4, charge_blocks 1. Held false.\n\nNow UAV_02 missions: MISSION_02 (seq 0), MISSION_03 (seq 1), MISSION_04 (seq 2). All assigned to UAV_02, start at VP_01.\n\nUAV_02: initial SOC 9.635464 kWh (no override, note baseline decision had depart_slot 4 with charge_blocks 0). Max wind level 1, so must choose paths with max_wind_level <=1. battery capacity 16, charge 1.2 per block, reserve 1.5, empty energy per km 0.1, payload energy 0.026 per kg per km. payload 0.5 kg for all missions, so payload_energy_per_km = 0.012? Actually base: empty_energy_kwh_per_km 0.1, payload_energy_kwh_per_km_kg 0.026. So for 0.5 kg, energy increase = 0.5*0.026 = 0.013 per km. So total energy per km = 0.1 + 0.013 = 0.113 kWh/km. But the path options already give computed energy_kwh based on distance, wind, etc. Probably already includes payload. Check MISSION_02 path: PATH_07 distance 1.08, empty_energy: 0.1*1.08=0.108, payload_energy: 0.5*0.026*1.08=0.01404, sum=0.12204, but path energy_kwh=0.135464, which is higher due to wind? wind multiplier: wind_energy_multiplier_per_level 0.11 per level. For wind level 1, multiplier = 1 + 0.11 = 1.11. So 0.12204*1.11 = 0.135464, matches. So path energy is total.\n\nMISSION_02: origin VP_01, dest VP_04, release 4, due 27. Path options: PATH_07, travel slots 2, max wind 1, energy 0.135464, corridor COR_05. Check closures: COR_05 is not listed in closed_corridor_slots at all! So open always. So PATH_07 is fine. Need to check reserve after flight. Initial SOC 9.635464, no deadhead (origin VP_01, start at VP_01), service flight energy 0.135464, reserve 1.5. SOC after = 9.635464 - 0.135464 = 9.5 >1.5, ok. Then turnaround and charge at VP_04. Then MISSION_03 origin VP_04, so after turnaround, UAV at VP_04. So for MISSION_03, requires no deadhead (since start at VP_04). So we can schedule MISSION_02 at depart_slot. Baseline had depart 4, path PATH_07, charge 0. With depart 4, travel 2 slots, arrival 6. Turnaround slot 7. Then release for MISSION_03 is 7, so can depart as early as 7. But we must ensure pad capacity, charger capacity. Also, MISSION_02 due 27, plenty of time. But MISSION_03 is urgent with due 10, so we need to depart as early as possible to meet due. If we depart MISSION_03 at 7, we need to check path options and energy.\n\nMISSION_03: UAV_02, origin VP_04, dest VP_05, release 7, due 10, payload 0.5 kg. Path options:\n- PATH_04: travel slots 2, distance 1.0, energy 0.12543, max wind 1, corridor COR_01. Check COR_01 closures: closed slots 7-30 (all from 7 to 30). So if depart at d, corridor occupied at d and d+1. If d=7, then slots 7,8 are closed, so not allowed. If d=8, slots 8,9 closed. d=9: 9,10 closed. d=10: 10,11 closed, etc. Actually, COR_01 is closed for all slots from 7 to 30 inclusive. So any departure from 7 onwards would hit closed slots. So PATH_04 is infeasible for any depart after 7. PATH_06: travel slots 1, corridor COR_09, max wind 1, energy 0.12543 (same energy, presumably). COR_09 closures: not listed at all! So PATH_06 is open. Travel 1 slot, so if depart at d, only slot d is occupied. So PATH_06 is feasible. PATH_09: travel slots 4, corridor COR_02, max wind 0, energy 0.226, travel time 4 slots. COR_02 not closed. But max wind 0, UAV_02 max wind 1 so fine. But travel 4 slots from 7 arrival? depart 7, arrival 11 > due 10, so fails due. So only PATH_06 is possible, with depart slot such that arrival <=10. Travel 1 slot, so arrival = depart + 1. To meet due 10, depart <=9. The earliest depart after MISSION_02 turnaround: MISSION_02 depart 4, travel 2 -> arrival 6, turnaround 7. So the UAV is free at slot 8? Turnaround takes 1 slot: if arrival at slot 6, turnaround at slot 7, then the UAV is ready for next mission at slot 8. But release is 7, but can't depart before available. So earliest depart for MISSION_03 is 8. With travel 1 slot, arrival 9 <=10, works. So depart_slot 8, path PATH_06. Check energy: After MISSION_02, we had SOC 9.5, no charge (if charge 0). Then MISSION_03 energy 0.12543, so SOC 9.5 - 0.12543 = 9.37457 > reserve 1.5. So no charge needed. But then MISSION_03 destination VP_05, turnaround 1 slot, then next mission MISSION_04 release 15, due 24. So that works.\n\nBut we must check pad capacity and charger capacity. At VP_04, UAV_02 will land at slot 6, turnaround at 7, freeing pad at 8. UAV_01 also does turnaround at VP_04: MISSION_01 depart 4, travel 2, arrival 6, turnaround 7 (maybe same slot?), so both UAVs at VP_04 might conflict. Pad capacity at VP_04 is 2, so two UAVs can occupy pads simultaneously. So both at slot 7 is fine (two pads). Then UAV_01 does post-service charge: charge_blocks 1, which occupies a charger at VP_04. Charging occurs after turnaround. Turnaround at 7, charge block probably at slot 8? Or is charging concurrent with turnaround? Typically, charging happens in designated charger slots, separate from pad. But the UAV must be on ground, after turnaround. Usually, after turnaround, the UAV moves to a charger (if needed) and charges for charge_blocks slots. This occupies a charger resource. At VP_04, charger capacity 2. UAV_01 will need a charger for 1 slot at slot 8? But what about UAV_02? It does not charge at VP_04 (charge_blocks 0), so it doesn't need a charger. So no conflict. So far okay.\n\nNow MISSION_04: UAV_02, origin VP_05, dest VP_02, release 15, due 24, payload 0.5 kg. Path options: PATH_03, travel slots 1, max wind 0, energy 0.15255, corridor COR_07. COR_07 closures? Not listed, open. Travel 1 slot. So after MISSION_03, UAV_02 at VP_05 after turnaround. MISSION_03 depart 8, travel 1 slot -> arrival 9? If depart=8, travel 1 slot, arrival = 8+1=9? Or arrival slot = depart + travel slots = 9. Turnaround at 10. Then ready for MISSION_04 at 11. But release 15, so must depart at >=15. So we could depart at 15. With travel 1 slot, arrival 16, due 24, fine. Energy: after MISSION_03, SOC 9.37457, minus 0.15255 = 9.22202 >1.5, ok. So MISSION_04: held false, path PATH_03, depart 15, charge 0. But we need to ensure that UAV_02 does not run out of energy for later missions? No more missions. So that's fine. However, we need to check if any intervention overrides affect: initial_soc_overrides for UAV_02 is none, so 9.635464 is correct. The pad_capacity_overrides: pad_capacity at VP_01 slot 4 is overridden to 1. So at slot 4, only one pad available at VP_01. Both UAV_01 and UAV_02 plan to depart from VP_01 at slot 4? UAV_01 MISSION_01 departure at 4 from VP_01 (if we set) uses a pad. UAV_02 MISSION_02 departure at 4 from VP_01 also uses a pad. That would be 2 UAVs departing, but pad capacity at VP_01 slot 4 is 1. So conflict! So we cannot have both depart at slot 4. We need to adjust. The baseline had MISSION_01 depart 4 (UAV_01) and MISSION_02 depart 4 (UAV_02). But now with pad capacity override to 1, they can't both depart at 4. We must delay one. Priority: lexicographic optimization: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy. But we are not supposed to hold missions unless necessary? The baseline probably had 0 holds. Here we might need to delay a departure, which is not a hold; hold is a separate flag. If we change departure slot, it's not a hold. So we can adjust depart_slot of MISSION_02 to later slot, but must still satisfy due and release. MISSION_02 release 4, due 27. So we can delay its departure until pad is free. At VP_01 pad capacity default 2, but override for slot 4 makes it 1. What about later slots? The override only mentions slot 4: {\"capacity\":1,\"slot\":4,\"vertiport_id\":\"VP_01\"}. So for slot 4, capacity 1; for other slots, default 2. So after slot 4, capacity 2. So we can have UAV_01 depart at 4, occupying the one pad at slot 4. UAV_02 can depart at 5, after pad capacity returns to 2 at slot 5? But check if pad capacity at slot 5 is 2 (default). So UAV_02 can depart at 5. That would delay MISSION_02 start by 1 slot. Depart 5, travel 2 slots -> arrival 7. Turnaround 8. Then MISSION_03 earliest depart 9. Still within release 7? But release is 7, so depart 9 >7, that violates release? Actually, release means the mission can start at or after that slot. So depart 9 after release 7 is allowed. But due 10 for MISSION_03, and travel 1 slot, arrival 10, meets due. So MISSION_03 depart 9, arrival 10, works. But we need to check energy: after MISSION_02, we'll have SOC after flight: initial 9.635464 - 0.135464 = 9.5 (unchanged). After turnaround at 8, SOC remains 9.5. Then MISSION_03 uses 0.12543, SOC after 9.37457, fine. Then MISSION_04 depart 15. So that sequence works. But we must also verify if any corridor closure affects MISSION_02's path at new departure. PATH_07 corridor COR_05 is open all slots, no problem. Also, need to check that UAV_02's available_slot is 4, so it can depart as early as 4, but no penalty for later.\n\nThus, a potential solution:\n- MISSION_01: uav=UAV_01, held=false, path=PATH_08, depart_slot=4, charge_blocks=1\n- MISSION_02: uav=UAV_02, held=false, path=PATH_07, depart_slot=5, charge_blocks=0\n- MISSION_03: uav=UAV_02, held=false, path=PATH_06, depart_slot=9, charge_blocks=0 (or maybe charge? Not needed)\n- MISSION_04: uav=UAV_02, held=false, path=PATH_03, depart_slot=15, charge_blocks=0\n- MISSION_05: uav=UAV_01, held=false, path=PATH_02, depart_slot=9, charge_blocks=0\n\nBut MISSION_05 depart slot 9: after MISSION_01, UAV_01 turn around at VP_04 arrived 6, turn 7, charge 1 block at slot 8, then free at slot 9. So depart 9 possible. Check if pad capacity at VP_04 for MISSION_05? UAV_02 also might be at VP_04 at slots? UAV_02 MISSION_02 arrives at VP_04 at slot 7 (depart 5 + travel 2). Turnaround at 8, then free at 9. So UAV_02 is at VP_04 at slot 7 (arrival), slot 8 turnaround, and leaves at 9? MISSION_03 depart 9 from VP_04, so it uses a pad at slot 9 for departure. So both UAV_01 and UAV_02 might want to depart from VP_04 at slot 9. Pad capacity VP_04 default 2, so fine. Charger capacity: UAV_01 charged at slot 8, one charger occupied. So no conflict.\n\nNow, check MISSION_03 path PATH_06: depart 9, travel 1 slot -> arrival slot 10. That meets due 10? Due is 10, arrival 10 satisfies \"by due slot\" as per baseline interpretation. So ok.\n\nNow, energy checks: UAV_01: \n- Start SOC 2.713174. MISSION_01 energy 0.144733 -> after flight 2.568441. Reserve check: >=2.5 ok. Turnaround, then charge 1 block of 0.95 -> SOC 3.518441. \n- MISSION_05 depart 9, energy 0.1208 -> after flight 3.397641. Reserve check: >=2.5 ok. No more missions. So good.\n\nUAV_02: \n- Start SOC 9.635464. MISSION_02 energy 0.135464 -> after 9.5. Reserve check >=1.5 ok. Turnaround. \n- MISSION_03 depart 9, energy 0.12543 -> after 9.37457. Reserve check ok. Turnaround at 10. \n- MISSION_04 depart 15, energy 0.15255 -> after 9.22202. Reserve ok. Done.\n\nNow check charge_blocks for MISSION_03: we set 0. No charging needed. But could we add charge? Not required, but we want to minimize total holds, then total holds is 0. No holds, so we are fine.\n\nNow we need to verify all constraints: corridor closures, pad/charger capacities, release/due windows, and intervention overrides.\n\nCorridor closures: \n- MISSION_01 PATH_08 COR_03 slots 4,5 (both open because closure starts at 6). OK.\n- MISSION_02 PATH_07 COR_05 slots 5,6 (open, no closures). OK.\n- MISSION_03 PATH_06 COR_09 slot 9 (open, no closures). OK.\n- MISSION_04 PATH_03 COR_07 slot 15 (open). OK.\n- MISSION_05 PATH_02 COR_04 slot 9 (open because closed only at 4-8,10-...; 9 is open). OK.\n\nPad capacities:\n- VP_01: slot 4: UAV_01 departs -> uses 1 pad (capacity 1, OK). No other departures at 4. Slot 5: UAV_02 departs, capacity 2, OK.\n- VP_04: \n  - arrival: UAV_01 arrival slot 6, UAV_02 arrival slot 7. At slot 6, only UAV_01, capacity 2, OK. At slot 7, UAV_01 turn? Turnaround likely occupies pad at slot 7, UAV_02 arrives slot 7, so two pads occupied? Actually, turnover: the UAV is on the ground at the pad during turnaround. So at slot 7, UAV_01 is still on pad (since it arrived at 6, does turnaround at 7). UAV_02 arrives at 7 and occupies a pad. So two pads used, capacity 2, OK.\n  - slot 8: UAV_01 charging? Charging may not occupy a pad; but turnaround finishes at 7, then UAV_01 moves to charger for slot 8. Pad is freed. UAV_02 turnaround at 8 (arrival 7, turnaround 8). So at slot 8, UAV_02 on pad, capacity 2, OK.\n  - departures: at slot 9, both UAV_01 and UAV_02 depart. But departure uses pad for the takeoff slot. So at slot 9, both need pad. Capacity 2, OK.\n- VP_05: UAV_03 (MISSION_03) arrives slot 10, turnaround slot 11, then free. No other traffic.\n- VP_03: MISSION_05 arrives slot 10? Actually MISSION_05 depart 9, travel 1, arrival 10. Turnaround 11? No next mission, so after turnaround it's done. Pad capacity OK.\n- VP_02: MISSION_04 arrives 16, turnaround 17, done.\n\nCharger capacities:\n- VP_01: no charging.\n- VP_04: UAV_01 charges at slot 8, 1 block. Charger count 2, OK.\n- Others: no charging.\n\nNow, check if any mission is held? No, all held false.\n\nLexicographic optimization: urgent holds (0 holds for urgent), total holds (0), repair distance (distance changes?), weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, energy. We are not required to optimize beyond feasibility? The task says \"Optimize the stated task lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\" So we must find the best solution according to these criteria. Our solution has 0 holds for all, so urgent holds and total holds minimal. Next, repair distance: distance is total distance flown? Or repair distance? The baseline had some distances. We changed MISSION_01 from PATH_05 (distance 1.2) to PATH_08 (0.9), so distance decreased. MISSION_02 kept PATH_07 (1.08). MISSION_03 baseline had PATH_04 (distance 1.0) but now we use PATH_06 (distance 1.0), same. MISSION_04 baseline PATH_03 (1.35), same. MISSION_05 PATH_02 (0.8) same. So total distance: 0.9 + 1.08 + 1.0 + 1.35 + 0.8 = 5.13 km. Baseline: MISSION_01 PATH_05 1.2, MISSION_02 PATH_07 1.08, MISSION_03 PATH_04 1.0, MISSION_04 PATH_03 1.35, MISSION_05 PATH_02 0.8 = 5.43 km. So we reduced distance, good. Weighted tardiness: all missions meet due, so tardiness 0. Delay plus charge wait: delay is waiting time before departure after release/available? There might be a penalty for departure delays. We delayed MISSION_02 by 1 slot (from 4 to 5), and MISSION_03 by 2 slots? Baseline MISSION_03 depart 7, now depart 9, so delay 2 slots. But baseline had MISSION_03 depart 7, but due 10, so not tardy. We have delay but no tardiness. The objective \"delay plus charge wait\" probably includes waiting for pad/charger (delay) and waiting for charging (charge wait). Our solution has some delay for MISSION_02 and MISSION_03, but we cannot avoid it due to p